 **Parameters:**
-- **Voting Threshold:** Not explicitly given in sources. A typical value might be 2 or a majority of views [S1].
-- **SAM Prompts:** User-specified points on the reference view [S1].
+*   **Point Prompts**: User-defined 2D coordinates on the reference view [S1].
+*   **SAM Model**: Segment Anything Model, used for mask generation [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Inefficiency:** The method may require multiple rounds of segmentation for certain scenes, as 2D masks from point prompts do not immediately generate a complete 3D segmentation [S1].
-- **Ambiguity:** If Gaussians project to multiple masked regions in different views, voting resolves conflicts.
-- **SAM Accuracy:** SAM may fail on certain textures or structures, leading to incorrect 2D masks [S1].
+*   **Inefficiency**: The method cannot immediately generate a complete 3D segmentation from a single 2D mask created with point prompts. It may require multiple rounds of segmentation for certain scenes, which is inefficient [S1].
+*   **Visibility**: Gaussians not visible in any training view or not covered by SAM masks will not be segmented.
+*   **Prompt Accuracy**: Inaccurate point prompts lead to incorrect 2D masks, propagating errors to the 3D segmentation [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### SA3D
-SA3D is a method for 3D object segmentation within 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) that leverages the Segment Anything Model (SAM) to generate initial 2D masks from text or point prompts in a single view [S1]. It distinguishes itself by alternating between mask inverse rendering and cross-view self-prompting to iteratively refine the 3D mask until complete object segmentation is achieved, avoiding the need for manual multi-view annotation [S1].
+SA3D is a method for 3D object segmentation within 3D Gaussian Splatting that leverages the Segment Anything Model (SAM) to generate initial 2D masks from text or point prompts in a single view [S1]. It distinguishes itself by alternating between mask inverse rendering and cross-view self-prompting to iteratively refine the 3D mask until complete object segmentation is achieved, avoiding the need for manual multi-view annotation [S1].
+
+### Fine-Level Affinity-Based Segmentation
+This variant refines coarse segmentation by learning affinity features from 2D masks generated by SAM, enabling fine-grained structure distinction [S2]. It lifts 2D masks to a 3D affinity field, which is then used to subdivide coarse-level color-based segmentation results [S2]. The affinity field network is trained using a loss function $\mathcal{L} = \mathcal{L}_{L2} + \lambda_1 \mathcal{L}_{TV} + \lambda_2 \mathcal{L}_{DSSIM}$, where $\mathcal{L}_{TV}$ is total variation loss and $\mathcal{L}_{DSSIM}$ is based on the structural similarity index measure (SSIM) between rendered and ground truth images [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of Gaussians $M$, views $V$, and SAM inference time. Rendering all views takes $O(V \cdot M)$. SAM inference is $O(V \cdot \text{image\_size})$. Voting is $O(M \cdot V)$.
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(M)$ for storing labels and vote counts.
-- **Performance:** Real-time PSE is supported during inference [S1]. Rendering times vary by dataset, e.g., 29.68 ms for 256x256x256 resolution (five jets dataset) [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Training-free, flexible partial scene editing, supports color, opacity, and lighting adjustments [S1].
-- **Limitations:** May require multiple rounds of segmentation for complex scenes [S1]. SAM accuracy impacts results [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity**: Depends on the number of Gaussians $M$, the number of training views $N$, and the cost of SAM inference. For each view, SAM inference and projection checks are performed.
+*   **Memory Complexity**: Requires storing the Gaussian primitives and vote counts. No additional large learnable feature vectors are required per Gaussian in the basic voting approach [S1], unlike methods that optimize learnable features [S1].
+*   **Strengths**: Training-free; does not require modifying the base Gaussian representation (e.g., adding learnable features); supports real-time partial scene editing (PSE) after segmentation [S1].
+*   **Limitations**: Inefficient for scenes requiring multiple segmentation rounds; relies on SAM's accuracy; requires point prompts for each segment [S1].
+*   **When to use**: When rapid, training-free segmentation is needed for partial editing of a pre-trained 3DGS scene [S1].
+*   **When not to use**: When high-precision fine-grained segmentation is required without manual prompting (consider affinity-based methods) or when automatic text-driven segmentation without iterative refinement is needed (consider SA3D) [S1, S2].
 
 ## Background
-The method is introduced in TexGS-VolVis, a framework for expressive scene editing in volume visualization using 2D Gaussian Splatting [S1]. It builds on the idea of 2D-lift-3D segmentation, where 2D masks from foundation models are lifted to 3D to segment Gaussian primitives [S1]. This allows for partial scene editing (PSE) and non-photorealistic scene editing (NPSE) [S1].
+The method was introduced in the context of TexGS-VolVis, a framework for expressive scene editing in volume visualization using 2D Gaussian Splatting [S1]. It builds on prior work in 2D-lift-3D segmentation [5, 8, 25, 35] and adapts the training-free strategy inspired by SAGD [25] for volumetric data [S1]. The approach enables partial scene editing (PSE) and non-photorealistic scene editing (NPSE) by decomposing scenes into editable Gaussian groups [S1].
 
